        "He didn't charge at me or throw another punch. He grabbed his shoes, jacket, and the small leather duffle bag he'd brought back from Hawaii. The belt hung in his hand for half a second, then he dropped it to the ground, as if it was no longer a weapon but irrefutable evidence of guilt.",
        "\"You're going to regret it,\" he said, his voice weak.",
        "\"No,\" I said softly, \"I think I finally don't have to regret my past choices anymore.\"",
        "He walked out of the bedroom, down the hallway, and toward the door. The sound of every step seemed so familiar. It had only been six weeks since he had moved into my house, but it was as if he had filled every corner with his presence—the smell of his cologne in the bathroom, his watch on the dresser, his preferred habits in the kitchen, and his rules that tried to keep me locked in.",
        "The door opens.",
        "Then close.",
        "I'm alone.",
        "Although it is not completely safe yet.",
        "But at least I'm free.",
        "This is important.",
        "I stood in the middle of the bedroom for a long time, boxing gloves still on my hands, the residual heat of the adrenaline still lingering under my skin. The suitcase was lying open beside the bed, and a blue sundress slipped out from the edge - the same dress that Ethan said was \"too conspicuous\" during dinner in Maui. I remember apologizing while carefully folding it into my suitcase, thinking that compromise was an essential part of running a marriage.",
        "I made a breathy sound - I couldn't tell whether I was laughing or crying.",
        "My cell phone rang.",
        "An unfamiliar number was displayed on the screen. My heart skipped a beat, but I picked up anyway.",
        "\"This is the 911 dispatch center,\" a calm female voice said, \"Are you safe now?\"",
        "I swallowed. \"I think so. My husband left home.\"",
        "\"Are there any weapons at the scene?\"",
        "I looked at the belt on the floor.",
        "\"There's no gun,\" I said. \"He had a belt, and he just tried to hit me with it.\"",
        "\"The police are on their way. Please stay on the line.\"",
        "## Chapter 3: When the Police Lights Arrived",
        "I sat on the edge of the bed, careful not to touch anything else around me. The dispatcher asked a lot of questions, and I answered them as clearly as I could: my name, Ethan's name, our address, if he had been drinking, if he had threatened me before, if I needed medical attention.",
        "\"No,\" I said, \"I'm not hurt.\"",
        "Then, after a brief silence, I added: \"Not physically.\"",
        "The dispatcher was in no hurry to fill in the blank with words.",
        "Sometimes kindness isn’t about what someone says to you, it’s about the space they leave for you to breathe.",
        "When the police car arrived, blue and red police lights flashed past the bedroom window, casting flickering light and shadow on the wall. Before opening the door, I took off my boxing gloves. Without the wrapping of the gloves, my hands looked extra small, real, and trembling slightly.",
        "Standing on the porch were two officers, a man and a woman. When the female police officer saw me, her eyes softened, but she didn't speak to me as if she were dealing with fragile glass.",
        "\"Mrs. Hale?\" she asked.",
        "I hesitated.",
        "This surname, although still so new, has been completely tainted, scratching a sting in my heart.",
        "\"Maya Bennett,\" I said. \"I have not had time to change everything yet.\"",
        "\"Ms. Bennett,\" she corrected gently, \"I'm Officer Reyes and this is Officer Donnelly. Can we come in?\"",
        "I stepped aside.",
        "They listened carefully to my story, took photos of the belt, recorded the broken lamp, surveillance cameras, saved audio recordings, and phone calls from Ethan’s mother. Officer Reyes asked me if I had somewhere I could go tonight.",
        "“This is my house,” I countered instinctively.",
        "She didn't argue with me: \"It is. But sometimes it feels better to be gone for a few hours.\"",
        "I looked around. Our wedding photos are displayed on the TV cabinet in the living room, still in silver frames. Ethan chose this photo because, in his words, I looked \"tender\" in it.",
        "I walked over, put the photo frame on the table, and picked up the car keys.",
        "\"My aunt's house is twenty minutes from here,\" I said.",
        "Officer Reyes nodded: \"Very good.\"",
        "Before I left, she handed me a business card with a case number and several helpline numbers printed on it. \"You'd better contact an attorney as soon as possible. And anyone involved with your finances - banks, property managers, accountants, anyone who has access to your assets.\"",
        "A chill ran down my spine.",
        "\"My property,\" I murmured under my breath.",
        "Officer Reyes looked at me with a look that was both professional and human: \"You mentioned it on the phone.\"",
        "I nodded: \"My father left it to me.\"",
        "\"Then contact anyone who can help you. Contact tonight, if you can.\"",
        "I almost told her that until my father was too weak from cancer to climb the stairs, he took care of everything in the house himself. I almost told her that before my father taught me to drive, he taught me how to read a rental book. I almost told her that after my father died, I buried myself in a pile of home repairs and lease contracts because grief seemed less overwhelming when it came with a mountain of bills.",
        "When the words came to my lips, I just said: \"I will.\"",
        "I quickly packed my belongings: a sweater, a pair of jeans, laptop, charger, and the folder on my dad’s old desk that held copies of the real estate documents. By some strange coincidence, I also took away the small wooden box on the top of the wardrobe - it was left by my father. It had a brass buckle but no key.",
        "I don't know why I took it.",
        "Maybe it's because the other things he left behind suddenly don't feel so safe anymore.",
        "Maybe it's because I want something of his to be closer to me.",
        "As I drove toward Aunt Lena's house, the Chicago I saw through the windshield looked very different. Under the pale morning light, the city took on a cool gray-silver tone. Delivery trucks roared down the street, joggers waited at crosswalks, and soft white mist rose from coffee shop vents.",
        "The world actually has the nerve to continue functioning.",
        "Before I rang the doorbell, Aunt Lena had already opened the door. She was wearing a nightgown, her silver hair was braided casually, and her face was full of tiredness and worry.",
        "She glanced at me and blurted out, \"Where is he?\"",
        "\"Gone,\" I said.",
        "She pulled me inside and hugged me tightly.",
        "At that moment, I finally completely collapsed.",
        "It was not the kind of beautiful tears seen in movies, nor was it a perfect teardrop streaking across the cheek. I cried like something was completely bursting inside my body and had to be let out all at once. Aunt Lena stood in the hall, hugging me tightly with the kind of unwavering patience that had tolerated me since I was sensible.",
        "When I could finally breathe smoothly, she took me into the kitchen and made me a cup of hot tea that I didn't want to drink, but forced myself to drink it anyway.",
        "\"Tell me everything,\" she said.",
        "So, I did it.",
        "I told everything about the belt, the recording, Ethan's mother, the real estate, and the conspiracy.",
        "Aunt Lena listened quietly without interrupting me. It wasn't until I repeated that sentence - \"As long as the property is transferred to your name, no one will care what happens to this family in the future\" - that her face suddenly darkened.",
        "## Chapter 4: The Name He Borrowed",
        "\"Your father saw through them.\"",
        "I looked up.",
        "The kitchen seemed to be spinning.",
        "\"What do you mean?\"",
        "Aunt Lena's expression changed instantly. Not really guilty, but with a kind of regret - the kind of regret that after waiting for many years, the fire can no longer be contained.",
        "\"Maya,\" she said slowly, \"there are some things that your father didn't plan to tell me unless he had to.\"",
